<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Case study with per-step logits.</h3>

<figure id="A4.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.22102v1/fig/screenshot_2024-10-27_21-07-29.png" id="A4.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/343;" width="548" height="343" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>
An image case from dataset ScreenSpot-Pro (ppt_windows/screenshot_2024-10-27_21-07-29.png)
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To better illustrate how VPSG corrects coordinate prediction bias, we analyze a single case from <span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Screenspot-Pro</span> (<span id="A4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ppt_windows/screenshot_2024-10-27_21-07-29.png</span>) <a href="#A4.F6" title="In D.2 Case study with per-step logits. ‣ Appendix D More Experiment Result ‣ Mitigating Coordinate Prediction Bias from Positional Encoding Failures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
The ground-truth bounding box center is <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[659,857]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>659,857</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[659,857]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The base model without guidance predicts <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1024,856]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1024,856</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1024,856]</annotation></semantics></math>, while VPSG successfully outputs the correct <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[659,857]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>659,857</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[659,857]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Table <a href="#A4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ D.2 Case study with per-step logits. ‣ Appendix D More Experiment Result ‣ Mitigating Coordinate Prediction Bias from Positional Encoding Failures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> lists the top-10 logits probabilities at each decoding step.
At the earliest <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-digit steps, the uncorrected model shows a strong bias toward larger numbers (e.g., tokens “1” and “0” dominate), reflecting spurious numeric priors induced by missing or unreliable positional encodings.
VPSG integrates negative evidence from multiple shuffled PE runs and systematically downweights these spurious peaks, allowing the true digit sequence to emerge and stabilizing the final <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x,y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x,y]</annotation></semantics></math> prediction.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>
Per-step top-1 logits probabilities for each decoded token in a representative example.
The base model without guidance drifts toward spurious large-<math id="A4.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> digits (e.g., “1”, “0” early), yielding an incorrect coordinate <math id="A4.T5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1024,856]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1024,856</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1024,856]</annotation></semantics></math>.
VPSG, by integrating negative evidence from multiple shuffled PE runs, suppresses these biased peaks and converges to the correct coordinate <math id="A4.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[659,857]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>659,857</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[659,857]</annotation></semantics></math>.
This example highlights how VPSG stabilizes numeric decoding and restores faithful spatial grounding.
</figcaption>
